      { question: "Is indoor cheaper than buying at a Maine dispensary?", answer: "Yes, after the first year. Year 1 indoor cost per ounce: $60-165/oz (equipment amortized). Ongoing indoor cost: $13-28/oz. Maine dispensary retail: $200-400/oz. The break-even point is 2-3 harvests, or approximately 6-9 months. Outdoor is even cheaper per ounce ($7-15/oz) but indoor gives you year-round harvests, photoperiod control, and protection from Maine's October humidity and bud-rot risk." },
      { question: "What if I lose power during a Maine winter storm?", answer: "A 1500VA UPS ($100-200) keeps lights running 20-45 minutes — enough for a graceful shutdown. A Jackery/EcoFlow 1000Wh station runs a 300W light for 2-3 hours. For multi-day outage protection, a Honda EU2200i generator ($1,000-2,000) is the robust solution. In a heated Maine basement, a 12-24 hour outage will stress but not kill plants if ambient temp stays above 55°F. CMP and Versant both publish outage maps; major ice storms in 2023 and 2024 caused multi-day outages across central and northern Maine — a small battery bank is the minimum sensible precaution." },
      { question: "Do I need a permit to grow cannabis indoors in Maine?", answer: "No. Personal home cultivation under <a href=\"https://legislature.maine.gov/legis/bills/display_ps.asp?LD=555&snum=131\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">LD 555 (131st Legislature, 2023)</a> / Title 28-B does not require a state permit. You must comply with plant-tag labeling rules (each plant labeled with owner name and date), keep plants out of public view, and have landowner permission if renting (per <a href=\"https://legislature.maine.gov/legis/bills/bills_128th/billtexts/HP057901.asp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">LD 799, 128th Legislature, 2017</a>). Municipalities may have additional zoning rules; check with your city/town clerk. Commercial cultivation requires a Maine OCP-issued cultivation license; renting out a grow space for payment is commercial cultivation and unlicensed commercial cultivation carries significant penalties." },
      { question: "Should I run my grow lights during off-peak hours to save on CMP's TOU rate?", answer: "Yes, if you enroll in CMP's optional Time-of-Use rate (effective January 2026). Running lights from 9 PM to 9 AM (all off-peak) means zero on-peak delivery charges. Set your timer to start at 9 PM. The supply charge (the largest portion of your bill) does not vary by time, but the delivery savings are real — the off-peak delivery rate is $0.067/kWh vs. $0.503/kWh on-peak. The TOU rate benefits you if ≥86% of usage is off-peak, which is achievable with a 12/12 or 18/6 schedule timed to start at 9 PM." }
    ]} />
